lemen love beef just  love everything about it  yeah okay so to play us out let's talk  about sourdough bread so if you've been  following me on Instagram you know I've  been getting really into bread baking  recently and I recently made the shift  over to sourdough and so sourdough is is  good I personally think it's tasty and  for some people who have like fodmap  sensitivities sourdough bread is a lot  more digestible and causes less stomach  problems than bread made with commercial  yeast because basically to make  sourdough work it needs a longer  fermentation time and that allows time  to break down a lot of those compounds  that people with fodmap sensitivities  struggle to digest so it's you know  theoretically maybe a little bit  healthier I think subjectively it's much  more delish  and if he struggled with bread just  consuming it and not having GI issues  sourdough may be good for you whereas  conventionally baked bread wanted so  anyway that's just some background about  sourdough so here here's some sourdough  tips if you want to get into baking  sourdough bread at home so to start with  if you want to do a real sourdough bread  you need a sourdough starter what I mean  by real sourdough bread is you can make  a cheating version a pale simulacrum of  sourdough bread by basically just adding  vinegar or something sour to regular  bread dough some of the acetic acid from  the vinegar will stick around and leave  the bread tasting sour that is  apparently how at least some like  mass-manufactured sourdough bread does  it just by adding sour things into the  dough instead of using a natural  sourdough starter but if you want to  make a real sourdough bread that means  you need a sourdough starter and that's  essentially just a mixture of flour and  water that picks up yeast and lactic  acid bacteria the yeast come from the  flower itself it's just hanging out on  the flower and small amounts and then it  gets to work breaking down the sugars  and multiplies and you have a nice  little colony of yeast and the lactic  acid bacteria as I understand it comes  from the air like from the environment  and doesn't come with the flour so you  need to get it you need to get a starter  going there's a lot of articles online  about how to maintain a starter it's  pretty straightforward you just discard  about two-thirds to half of it every day  and then feed it by adding equal parts  in mass wise of flour and water to it  mix it up let it go another 24 hours  dump half of it out add flour and water  again and once the starter is stable and  healthy you can keep it alive like that  virtually forever but the challenging  part where the starter is getting and  going in the first place and I've  actually tried to make sourdough breads  several times before but the thing I  always struggled with was getting a  healthy and productive starter going  in the issues so the issues you can run  into is either one not getting enough  lactic acid bacteria in the the starter  mix early enough or not getting enough  yeast and early enough because basically  once you get enough lactic acid bacteria  in the mix and once you get enough yeast  in the mix they form a symbiotic  relationship where you know they break  down different parts of the flower they  liberate food for the other ones to  consume it's a nice big happy family and  they also both like acidic conditions so  a pH somewhere between three point five  to five and once the pH drops to that  level that keeps harmful bacteria from  being able to grow so if pH gets too  high you could get an infection with  botulinum toxin and that can kill you  and that's not good do you want a  relatively low pH and so I struggled  with this before because essentially I'd  get to work on the feeding for a few  days and then I think basically there  just wasn't enough yeast in the flour I  was using or I wasn't getting quick  enough culturing of the lactic acid  bacteria to kill off the bad stuff and I  